Meralgia Paresthetica
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Anterolateral thigh numbness/burning, Exacerbation with standing/hip extension, Complete absence of motor weakness, Positive Tinel's sign over inguinal ligament

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-032
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe burning pain and numbness in the thigh leading to an inability to stand or walk, with intense fear of a severe spinal nerve compression.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with sudden, severe unilateral neuropathic pain and numbness in the lower limb, mimicking an acute lumbar radiculopathy (sciatica) or spinal cord lesion. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to perform a focused neurological examination, definitively exclude an acute motor deficit or cauda equina syndrome, and provide immediate neuropathic pain relief before the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The outside of my thigh is completely numb, but at the same time it feels like it's being burned with a hot iron every time I stand up straight."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 3-day history of localized numbness, tingling, and severe, distinct burning neuropathic pain strictly over the anterolateral aspect of the right thigh. The symptoms acutely worsened today after an 8-hour shift wearing a heavy, tight toolbelt. The patient notes severe exacerbation of the burning by prolonged standing or extension of the hip (walking). Conversely, there is profound relief of symptoms when sitting or flexing the hip. The skin over the lateral thigh is distinctly hypersensitive (allodynia) to light touch (clothing brushing against it). The patient explicitly denies back pain or loss of bowel/bladder control.
Functional Status: Currently sitting with the right hip flexed; unwilling to stand due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding permanent nerve damage or need for spinal surgery.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting weight to avoid extending the right hip.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals a complete and absolute absence of objective motor weakness in the bilateral lower limbs (hip flexion, knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion/plantarflexion all 5/5). Deep tendon reflexes at the knee and ankle are completely normal and symmetrical. Sensation to light touch is markedly decreased, yet painful (dysesthesia), strictly in the distribution of the right lateral femoral cutaneous nerve. A sharply positive Tinel's sign is reproducible by tapping directly over the lateral end of the inguinal ligament, sending a shock down the outer thigh. Documented history of entirely normal lumbar spine imaging.
Environmental Observations: Heavy leather toolbelt visible on the floor, corresponding to the mechanical trigger.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Meralgia Paresthetica - Status: Acute mechanical compression flare
Differentials:

L2/L3 Lumbar Radiculopathy - Ruled out clinically by the completely normal motor exam (no hip flexor/quadriceps weakness), normal knee jerk reflex, and strictly normal back exam.

Peripheral Neuropathy (e.g., Diabetic) - Ruled out by the strictly unilateral, focal distribution rather than a bilateral 'stocking-glove' pattern.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out by the superficial burning neuropathic pain, lack of limb swelling, and normal peripheral pulses.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to severe, acute neuropathic limb pain mimicking an acute spinal radiculopathy or disc herniation. The intensity of the pain and the patient's acute inability to stand upright necessitated an immediate neurological examination. By confirming entirely normal motor power and reflexes, and localizing the nerve compression specifically to the superficial inguinal ligament via a positive Tinel's sign, the physician definitively ruled out a severe structural spinal emergency. This immediate clinical diagnosis safely de-escalated the patient's anxiety and prevented a highly inappropriate emergency department presentation, fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised immediate and absolute cessation of wearing the heavy, restrictive toolbelt. Prescribed a short course of topical Lidocaine 5% patches to immediately numb the hypersensitive skin area, and oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS to reduce local inflammation at the inguinal ligament.
Positive Behavioral Change: Immediately transition to using heavy-duty suspenders to carry work tools rather than a tight, waist-bearing belt. Physically removing the mechanical compression directly from the inguinal ligament will rapidly decompress the trapped nerve, rewarding the patient with a significant reduction in burning pain and restoring the ability to stand upright comfortably.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Work suspenders.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 2 weeks to consider a local corticosteroid injection if conservative measures fail.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing sudden true weakness (inability to lift the leg or foot), loss of bowel or bladder control, or numbness around the saddle/groin area.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is based on a focal peripheral nerve entrapment; development of back pain or true weakness requires immediate reassessment.
Clinical Course: Meralgia paresthetica is a 'pinched nerve' strictly at the surface of the hip, not in the spine. It is intensely painful but mechanically benign. The nerve will recover fully once the physical pressure (tight clothing/belts) is permanently removed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if your leg actually becomes weak or gives way, if you develop severe back pain, or if you lose control of your bladder or bowels.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A purely sensory nerve runs just under the skin at the front of your hip. The heavy toolbelt has physically crushed this nerve against your pelvic bone, causing it to misfire and send severe 'burning' signals. Because it is a sensory nerve only, your muscles are perfectly safe.
Actionable Steps: Sleep with a pillow tucked under your knees tonight. Flexing your hips slightly while sleeping permanently takes the tension off the nerve, preventing the nerve from being stretched tight like a guitar string, which will help it heal faster.

